<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"/>
  <title>My Mother at Sixty-Six</title>
  <style>
    body {
      background-color: #121212;
      color: #e0e0e0;
      font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif;
      line-height: 1.6;
      padding: 2rem;
    }

    .container {
      max-width: 800px;
      margin: auto;
      background-color: #1e1e1e;
      padding: 2rem;
      border-radius: 10px;
      box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    }

    h1, h2 {
      color: #BB86FC;
    }

    p {
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }

    .highlight {
      color: #03dac6;
      font-style: italic;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>My Mother at Sixty-Six</h1>
    <h2>By Kamala Das</h2>

    <p>
      This autobiographical poem captures the <span class="highlight">deep emotional bond</span> between a daughter and her aging mother. On a drive to the Cochin airport, the poet looks at her mother, sitting beside her in the car, and realizes how pale, weak, and old she looks — like a "late winter’s moon."
    </p>

    <p>
      The sudden realization that her mother may not live long fills her with a sense of <span class="highlight">childhood fear</span> — the fear of separation and loss. She turns away and looks outside the window to distract herself from her emotions.
    </p>

    <p>
      The poet notices life and energy outside — children running and trees sprinting — a stark contrast to her mother’s lifeless appearance. This imagery conveys the contrast between <span class="highlight">youth and age, vitality and decay</span>.
    </p>

    <p>
      At the airport, while bidding goodbye, she hides her pain with a smile. Though she feels sorrowful and helpless, she smiles and says, “See you soon, Amma,” trying to hold onto hope and strength, even while knowing the truth.
    </p>

    <p>
      This poignant poem reflects <span class="highlight">love, loss, aging, and emotional vulnerability</span> — emotions many experience but rarely express so powerfully.
    </p>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
